.TH LS 1
.SH NAME
ls \- list contents of directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ls
[
.B \-dlp
] [
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
For each directory argument,
.I ls
lists the contents of the directory.
For each file argument,
.I ls
repeats its name and any other information requested.
When no argument is given, the current directory is listed.
The output is sorted by name.
The name of an entry in a listed directory is the argument joined to
the entry's name, so
.B ls /bin
prints
.BR /bin/cat .
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.B \-d
If the argument is a directory, list it, not its contents.
.TP
.B \-l
List in long format, giving the mode (see below),
the size in bytes, and the name of each file.
.TP
.B \-p
Print only the final path element of each file name.
.PD
.PP
The mode printed under the
.B \-l
option contains 10 characters.
The first is
.B d
if the entry is a directory and
.B \-
otherwise.
The last 9 are three sets of three bits,
for the owner, the group and everyone else,
and within each set the characters
.BR r ,
.B w
and
.B x
say that the file may be read, written or executed,
with
.B \-
where the permission is absent.
.PP
Unlike Plan 9's
.IR ls ,
this one prints no owner, no group and no time,
because the files it lists carry none.
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/ls/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR stat (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The exit status is empty,
.B "can't stat"
when an argument does not exist, or
.B "can't open"
when a directory could not be read.
The other arguments are still listed.
.SH BUGS
There is no
.B \-t
and no
.BR \-m ,
because a file has no time and no owner to sort or print.
A listing is not quoted for
.IR rc (1).
